diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** None — no faulty subsystem, daemon, interface, or policy was found.

**Mechanism:** The user's helpdesk ticket reports that the 172.16.30.x subnet (pc12/pc13/pc14 on switch3 via mswitch0) is unreachable from pc1 (10.5.0.1) in Campus 2. A full diagnostic sweep of the live network found no evidence supporting this claim at any layer.

**Localization:** Every relevant component was checked. mswitch0 advertises 172.16.30.0/24 into OSPF area 0.0.0.0 on its vlan30 interface (172.16.30.254/24). sshswitch correctly installs `O>* 172.16.30.0/24 [110/45] via 55.5.1.1` in its FIB, and mswitch0 correctly installs the return route `O>* 10.5.0.0/24 [110/45] via 10.2.2.1`. All 9 OSPF routers show 17/17 routes and Full adjacencies. A live ping from pc1 to 172.16.30.12 returned 0% packet loss with 2–7 ms RTT. No ACL rules, no TC shaping, no link flaps, no DHCP anomalies, and no device pressure were detected across all 28 devices.

**Root cause:** The 172.16.30.0/24 subnet is fully reachable from Campus 2 right now; there is no active or historically traceable fault. The user's reported outage is unconfirmed — it may have been a momentary transient (e.g., a brief OSPF reconvergence during lab startup) that self-resolved, or a client-side misdiagnosis.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the user experiences recurrence, capture packet traces or OSPF log output (`log file /var/log/frr/frr.log` on mswitch0 and router16) at the time of failure to establish a reproducible mechanism.
=== END ===